// SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
// lib/plat/android/push.fi -- NOTIFICATIONS AND AN OWN PUSH CONNECTION,
// WITHOUT FIREBASE.
//
// ================================================================= WHY
//
// The usual way for a message to wake a phone is Firebase Cloud Messaging:
// Google's server holds the connection, Google's library in the app. This
// file is the other way: the app holds ONE TCP connection to its own relay
// and shows a notification per line that arrives. What keeps that
// connection alive while the app is not on screen is a FOREGROUND SERVICE
// -- Android's own, documented mechanism, with a permanent notification
// ("Verbindung zum Relay aktiv") so the user can see and stop it.
//
// ========================================================= THE PIECES
//
//   org.firn.FirnService   a class of four methods, WRITTEN BY FIRN into
//                          classes.dex (tools/android/servicedex_main.fi).
//                          Its methods are `native` and land here:
//                          Java_org_firn_FirnService_onStartCommand & co.
//   push_start(host, port) called by the program on its app thread: writes
//                          firn-push.cfg into the app's files directory,
//                          asks for POST_NOTIFICATIONS (Android 13+), starts
//                          the service and tells the host to keep the
//                          program alive when the activity goes away.
//   the push thread        a bionic pthread started by the service:
//                          connect, read lines, one notification per line,
//                          reconnect every 3 s. It works without the
//                          activity -- Android may restart the service in a
//                          fresh process (START_STICKY), then there is no
//                          program at all, and the static initialiser of
//                          FirnService has loaded this library by itself.
//
// Everything here is #[no_gc]: it runs on Android's main thread (service
// callbacks) and on the push thread, where the collector must never run.
// Every local reference is deleted -- the push thread lives for days, and
// the table of local references of an attached thread holds 512.
//
// ============================================================ LIMITS
//
// L1 The relay address is a dotted IPv4 address (no name lookup here yet).
// L2 One line = one message, at most 1000 octets, UTF-8. Characters outside
//    the Basic Multilingual Plane become '?': JNI wants MODIFIED UTF-8.
// L3 No TLS: the connection is plain TCP. For anything but a test the
//    relay must speak TLS (lib/std/crypto has the pieces, not wired here).
import android.ndk
import android.jnicall
import android.activity

export {
    push_start, push_stop, push_notify, push_running, push_keep,
    Java_org_firn_FirnService_onStartCommand,
    Java_org_firn_FirnService_onBind,
    Java_org_firn_FirnService_onDestroy,
}

